"Arranges user activities and tasks into a narrative backbone with smaller options and coherent delivery slices beneath it.\n\nUse when: Maintain the big picture while planning incremental delivery. Find a small useful release that supports an end-to-end user goal.\n\nAvoid when: The primary need is a detailed dependency schedule or a flat priority ranking.\n\nVisual grammar: Activities and user tasks form a left-to-right backbone. Smaller stories or variations sit below their corresponding tasks. Horizontal slices define coherent outcomes for successive releases or learning steps.\n\nBuild: Frame the users, product boundary and desired outcomes. Walk through the user's activity and identify tasks, variations and exceptions. Slice the map around useful experiences and explicit learning goals.\n\nCheck: Each story supports a meaningful user task. A slice enables a coherent experience rather than isolated component work. Unusual paths and recovery needs remain represented.\n\nPitfalls: Treating vertical position as a universal numerical priority. Calling a collection of backend tasks a complete user experience.\n\nExample: A course booking map spans discovery, comparison, reservation and attendance, with a first slice supporting a simple complete booking.\n\nAlternatives: Customer journey map, Now-next-later roadmap."
